  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y+1}-1 & =3 \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y+1} & =4 \\\\ ln\u2061({e}^{y+1}) & =ln\u2061(4) \\\\ y+1 & =2ln\u2061(2) \\\\ y & =2ln\u2061(2)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=2ln\u2061(2)-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
